<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maragha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maragha is a city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. It served as an early capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a>. Hulagu founded a famous astronomical observatory there, directed by the scholar Nasir al-Din al-Tusi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maragha.jpg" alt="Maragha" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maragheh State University · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maraghe.city.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hulagu made <b>Maragha</b> in Azerbaijan his capital, later supplanted by Tabriz, and adopted the title “Ilkhan”, usually understood as a subordinate khan acknowledging the supremacy of the Great Khan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Astronomers at the observatory of <b>Maragha</b> in north-western Iran, founded in 1259, and Ibn al-Shatir in 14th-century Damascus built planetary models without the equant, the device of Ptolemy that philosophers found hardest to accept, and some of their geometrical techniques reappear in Copernicus, though how he came to…</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Ahmadil ibn Ibrahim, a Kurdish emir of the Rawwadid family, held <b>Maragheh</b> under the Seljuk Empire until he was killed by the Nizari Ismailis in 1116.</blockquote><a href="/state/ahmadili-dynasty">Ahmadili dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>After Nader Shah's death in 1747, the town of <b>Maragheh</b>, south of Tabriz, and its fertile district came under the hereditary control of khans of the Muqaddam tribe.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-maragheh">Khanate of Maragheh</a></li><li><blockquote>In 889 Muhammad ibn Abi'l-Saj was appointed governor of Azerbaijan and ruled it almost independently from <b>Maragha</b> and later Ardabil, founding a hereditary emirate under the Abbasid Caliphate.</blockquote><a href="/state/sajid-emirate">Sajid Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
